## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical integration of image provenance with runtime attestation, ensuring defense in depth for CNF deployments in multi-region clusters.

## Key Concepts
- Image signing with Cosign and KMS-backed keys
- SPDX SBOM association and SBOM validation
- In-cluster attestation service generating verifiable tokens
- Admission control enforcing both provenance and attestation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: basic attestation gate
function attest(imageDigest, token, sbom) {
  if (!verifySignature(imageDigest)) return false;
  if (!verifySBOM(sbom)) return false;
  if (!verifyToken(token)) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle key rotation and revocation in the KMS?
- How would you simulate attestation failures in a namespace to verify policy behavior?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to implement per-tenant routing through ConfigMaps, path rewriting, and versioning with safe defaults.

## Key Concepts
- Namespace-scoped ConfigMaps for feature flags
- Request rewriting via tenant header
- Versioning strategy and default fallback
- Minimal YAML manifests for end-to-end validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo rewrite function
function rewrite(req, tenantId, version, mapping) {
  const base = mapping[tenantId] || mapping['default'];
  req.url = `/v${version || '1'}/api${req.path}`;
  req.headers['Accept'] = 'application/json';
  req.headers['Host'] = base.host;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test unknown tenants and version upgrades?
- How to handle partial mappings and rollback?

## Why This Is Asked
The candidate demonstrates practical understanding of idempotence in multi-tenant CNFs via sidecar patterns and Redis TTLs.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotence and deduplication per tenant
- Redis SETNX with TTL for race-free dedupe
- Sidecar data flow and fallback handling

## Code Example
```go
// simplified sketch
package main
import "net/http"
func Handle(req *http.Request) {
  tenant := req.Header.Get("X-TENANT-ID")
  id := req.Header.Get("X-REQUEST-ID")
  key := tenant + ":" + id
  // pseudo Redis call with TTL 120s
  ok := redis.SetNX(key, "1", 120 * time.Second)
  if !ok {
    // respond 409 with {"error":"duplicate"}
    return
  }
  // forward to backend
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test dedupe behavior under high concurrency?
- How would you scale Redis for many tenants and ensure TTL correctness?
